.TH UBERTOOTH\-UTIL 1 "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.PP
.BR ubertooth-util (1) 
\- general purpose Ubertooth utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
ubertooth\-util [\-v] [\-V] [\-H] [\-\-host\-version] [\-I] [\-d[0\-1]] [\-l[0\-1]] [\-S] [\-r] [\-U<index|serial|all>] [\-a[0\-7]] [\-c[2402\-2480]] [\-C[0\-78]] [\-q[1\-225]] [\-t] [\-z[\-128..127]] [\-e] [\-m] [\-n] [\-f] [\-i] [\-b] [\-p] [\-s] [\-N] [\-x] [\-h]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.BR ubertooth-util (1) 
is a catch\-all tool for doing generally useful things
with, to, and about Ubertooth. To get firmware revision and compile
information, run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-vV
.fi
.RE
.PP
To fully reset Ubertooth, run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-r
.fi
.RE
.PP
Other options are available for modifying LED states, setting channel,
and performing miscellaneous radio\-related actions. Refer to the
[OPTIONS][] section for full details.
.PP
An option value can be attached or given as the next word: \fB\fC\-c2440\fR and
\fB\fC\-c 2440\fR both set the channel, and \fB\fC\-z\-90\fR and \fB\fC\-z \-90\fR both set the squelch.
Without a number after it, \fB\fC\-c\fR (and \fB\fC\-a\fR, \fB\fC\-C\fR, \fB\fC\-d\fR, \fB\fC\-l\fR, \fB\fC\-z\fR) reads the
current value instead.
.PP
The utility also includes a simple range test tool for use with two
Uberteeth. Refer to [RANGE TEST][] for full details.
.PP
Operations that put the device into a continuous mode \- \fB\fC\-I\fR, \fB\fC\-q\fR,
\fB\fC\-x\fR, \fB\fC\-t\fR, \fB\fC\-n\fR, and \fB\fC\-e\fR \- first stop any operation
already running on that device, so each starts from a known idle state.
Stopping a mode, with \fB\fC\-S\fR or by starting another, puts the board back
to idle: LEDs off, channel 2441 MHz and squelch \-120 dBm. The power amplifier
level set with \fB\fC\-a\fR is kept.
Each of them runs on the board and \fB\fCubertooth\-util\fR returns
immediately, so the same board stays usable from other commands meanwhile.
\fB\fC\-I\fR, \fB\fC\-q\fR, \fB\fC\-x\fR, \fB\fC\-t\fR and \fB\fC\-e\fR keep
running until \fB\fC\-S\fR stops them or another mode replaces them;
\fB\fC\-n\fR (range test) ends by itself when the test finishes.
.PP
\fB\fC\-I\fR (identify) blinks the USR, RX and TX LEDs together, about three
on\-off blinks a second, until it is stopped:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-U<serial> \-I      # start blinking
ubertooth\-util \-U<serial> \-S      # stop; the LEDs end off
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\fC\-I\fR needs firmware of this tree's API version and refuses any other,
saying so. The other options work with any firmware, so \fB\fC\-v\fR can still
read the version and \fB\fC\-f\fR can reflash it.
.PP
Only one mode option can be given per command: \fB\fC\-q\fR, \fB\fC\-n\fR, \fB\fC\-e\fR, \fB\fC\-t\fR, \fB\fC\-x\fR,
\fB\fC\-I\fR, \fB\fC\-i\fR (In\-System Programming, ISP) and \fB\fC\-f\fR (Device Firmware Upgrade,
DFU) each end the command, so a command with two or more of them is refused
before any device is opened, nothing is sent to the device, and the exit
status is 1:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ubertooth\-util \-q \-n
error: options \-q \-n cannot be combined; give only one of \-q \-n \-e \-t \-x \-I \-i \-f
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
Common options:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-v\fR :
get firmware revision number; exits non\-zero if the read fails
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-V\fR :
get compile info; exits non\-zero if the read fails
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-H\fR, \fB\fC\-\-host\-version\fR :
get host tools (libubertooth) version; works without a device
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-I\fR :
identify ubertooth device by flashing the USR, RX, and TX LEDs on the board;
the command returns at once and the blink runs until \fB\fC\-S\fR or any mode
option ends it, leaving the LEDs off (see LEDs doc)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-d[0\-1]\fR :
get/set all LEDs
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-l[0\-1]\fR :
get/set USR LED
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-S\fR :
stop current operation
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-r\fR :
full reset; then waits up to 10 seconds for the same device (by serial with \fB\fC\-U<serial>\fR) to come back on USB before any later option runs, and exits non\-zero with \fB\fCerror: Ubertooth device <selector> did not come back within 10 s after the reset\fR if it does not. With several boards attached, select by serial: the index order can change across a reset
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-U<index|serial>\fR :
select which Ubertooth to use, either by USB enumeration index or by its serial number string; see SELECTING A DEVICE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-U all\fR :
report the read\-only queries \fB\fC\-b\fR, \fB\fC\-v\fR, \fB\fC\-V\fR, \fB\fC\-p\fR and \fB\fC\-s\fR for every attached Ubertooth; refused with any other option; see SELECTING A DEVICE
.RE
.PP
Radio options:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-a[0\-7]\fR :
get/set power amplifier level, 0 (lowest, \-25 dBm) to 7 (maximum, 0 dBm) at
the CC2400 radio\. The board powers on and resets (\fB\fC\-r\fR) at level 7, the
CC2400's own reset value (the firmware does not set it at start\-up)\.
Receiving does not change it\. Transmit modes use the level set here and
leave it unchanged: \fB\fC\-t\fR (TX test), Bluetooth and BLE transmit, and BLE and
E\-GO jamming\. The range test and repeater (\fB\fC\-n\fR, \fB\fC\-e\fR) step through levels
by design and leave the level wherever their sweep ended, so set it again
after using them\.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-c[2402\-2480]\fR :
get/set channel in MHz
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-C[0\-78]\fR :
get/set channel
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-q[1\-225 (RSSI threshold)]\fR :
start LED spectrum analyzer\. The threshold is \-N dBm, so a larger N lights
the LEDs for weaker signals; the default, bare \fB\fC\-q\fR, is 225 and lights
them for anything the radio reads\. The TX, RX and USR LEDs stand for 2412,
2437 and 2462 MHz\. \fB\fC\-S\fR stops it and leaves the LEDs off\.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-t\fR :
intitiate continuous transmit test
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-z[\-128..127]\fR :
get/set squelch level (a signed RSSI threshold; values above 0 are relative
to the average RSSI) for a later RX capture\.
Stopping a running mode resets it to the default, so \fB\fC\-z\fR cannot be
combined with a mode option (\fB\fC\-q\fR, \fB\fC\-n\fR, \fB\fC\-e\fR, \fB\fC\-t\fR, \fB\fC\-x\fR, \fB\fC\-I\fR, \fB\fC\-i\fR,
\fB\fC\-f\fR)\.
.RE
.PP
Range test:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-e\fR :
start repeater mode
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-m\fR :
display range test result
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-n\fR :
initiate range test
.RE
.PP
Miscellaneous:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-f\fR :
activate flash programming (DFU) mode
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-i\fR :
activate In\-System Programming (ISP) mode
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-b\fR :
get hardware board id number
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-p\fR :
get microcontroller Part ID (32\-bit, hexadecimal); exits non\-zero if the
device reports a nonzero status for the read
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC\-s\fR :
get microcontroller serial number
.RE
.SH SELECTING A DEVICE
.PP
With a single Ubertooth attached, the tools find it automatically and \fB\fC\-U\fR
is unnecessary.
.PP
With multiple Ubertooths attached, \fB\fC\-U\fR selects one either by its
position in the USB device list or by its serial number. \fB\fC\-U<n>\fR
selects by index (\fB\fC\-U0\fR is the first match, and so on); this index
reflects USB enumeration order (bus/port and plug order) \- it is not derived
from the serial number and is not guaranteed stable across reboots or
re\-plugging. \fB\fC\-U<serial>\fR selects the device whose serial number
matches the given string, which is stable across reboots and re\-plugging and
is the recommended way to pin a specific board.
.PP
The argument may be attached or separated \- \fB\fC\-U0\fR and \fB\fC\-U 0\fR are
equivalent, as are \fB\fC\-U<serial>\fR and \fB\fC\-U <serial>\fR. An all\-digits
argument is treated as an index, anything else as a serial. There is no fixed
upper limit on attached devices and the index is not capped at any particular
number (older docs showed \fB\fC\-U<0\-7>\fR, a convention only); with many
devices prefer serial selection.
.PP
An index must be one that is actually attached: it is a position in the list
of attached Ubertooths, so with \fIn\fR attached only \fB\fC\-U0\fR to
\fB\fC\-U<n\-1>\fR select a device. Any other index is refused before any
device is opened, and the tool exits non\-zero:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ubertooth\-util \-U2 \-s
error: no Ubertooth at index 2 (2 attached)
indexes change when boards are added or removed; select a board by serial with \-U<serial>
could not open Ubertooth device
.fi
.RE
.PP
Indexes are positions in the list of boards attached when the command runs:
unplugging \fB\fC\-U0\fR makes the old \fB\fC\-U1\fR the new \fB\fC\-U0\fR\&. To keep targeting the same
board, select it by serial number (or a unique part of it).
.PP
This includes a nonzero index with a single Ubertooth attached (\fB\fC\-U1\fR
of one device is refused; use \fB\fC\-U0\fR, or no \fB\fC\-U\fR at all). With no
Ubertooth attached, any \fB\fC\-U<n>\fR reports that no device could be opened.
A digit string too large to be a device index at all is refused the same way,
naming it as given:
\fB\fCerror: no Ubertooth at index 4294967296 (2 attached)\fR\&.
.PP
With several Ubertooths attached and no \fB\fC\-U\fR, the read\-only identity
queries \fB\fC\-s\fR (serial), \fB\fC\-v\fR (firmware version), \fB\fC\-V\fR (compile
info), \fB\fC\-p\fR (part ID) and \fB\fC\-b\fR (board ID) report every device, in
enumeration order, instead of refusing to pick one. A single query prints one
\fB\fC<index>: <value>\fR line per device:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ubertooth\-util \-s
0: 1cc001038088cb956c43ad61c52000f5
1: 05f00014e380df95f4436562c32000f5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Several queries together (for example \fB\fC\-vVspb\fR) print each report line
prefixed with the device index. A device that cannot be opened is shown as
\fB\fC<index>: <unavailable>\fR, a failed read as \fB\fC<index>: <query read
failed>\fR, and a query that reports an error as that error (for example
\fB\fC<error: unknown board ID 7>\fR). The other devices are still reported, and
the command exits non\-zero if any device failed. A failed \fB\fC\-v\fR or
\fB\fC\-V\fR read is shown as \fB\fC<index>: <firmware read failed>\fR or
\fB\fC<index>: <compile info read failed>\fR; a firmware version whose API
version cannot be read is shown without it and also makes the command fail.
Listing does not stop a mode a device is running. Any other option in the
same command still needs \fB\fC\-U\fR\&.
.PP
\fB\fC\-U all\fR asks for the same listing explicitly. It works with any number
of attached devices, including one (\fB\fC0: <value>\fR), and fails with
\fB\fCerror: no Ubertooth devices found\fR when none is attached:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ubertooth\-util \-U all \-s
0: 1cc001038088cb956c43ad61c52000f5
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\fC\-U all\fR only applies to the read\-only queries \fB\fC\-b\fR, \fB\fC\-v\fR,
\fB\fC\-V\fR, \fB\fC\-p\fR and \fB\fC\-s\fR\&. Combined with any other option (a
setter, a mode such as \fB\fC\-x\fR or \fB\fC\-q\fR, \fB\fC\-r\fR, \fB\fC\-S\fR,
\fB\fC\-N\fR, or another read such as \fB\fC\-c\fR), or given with no query at
all, it is refused before any device is opened:
.PP
.RS
.nf
error: \-U all only supports the read\-only queries \-b, \-v, \-V, \-p and \-s; select one device with \-U<index|serial> for other options
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only the exact word \fB\fCall\fR has this meaning. The other tools do not
support \fB\fC\-U all\fR; they treat it as a serial number and report that no
device matches.
.PP
To tell which index or serial is which physical device: run
\fB\fCubertooth\-util \-s\fR, or any other tool with no \fB\fC\-U\fR (with more than
one present it lists each index with its serial number), or flash the LEDs on a given device and watch which board blinks, then stop it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-U0 \-I
ubertooth\-util \-U0 \-S
.fi
.RE
.PP
For a stable identity across reboots, select a device by its serial number
(\fB\fC\-U<serial> \-I\fR); unlike the index, the serial does not change.
.SH RANGE TEST
.PP
Using two Uberteeth it is possible to perform a range test. One
Ubertooth acts as a repeater by running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-e
.fi
.RE
.PP
A second Ubertooth (the sender) initiates the range test by running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-n
.fi
.RE
.PP
During the range test the sender Ubertooth will transmit data packets at
increasing power level, listening for repeats from the repeater
Ubertooth. The receiving Ubertooth will validate that the received data
was not corrupted in flight.
.PP
The range test results can be displayed on the sender Ubertooth's system
by running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ubertooth\-util \-m
.fi
.RE
.PP
Before a range test has completed since the board started, \fB\fC\-m\fR prints
\fB\fCno range test result yet\fR and exits 0. A test whose packets did not match prints
\fB\fCInvalid range test: mismatch on byte <n>\fR\&.
.PP
Note that the range test transmits the Cortex M3 microcontroller's
serial number. If you do not wish to broadcast that information
wirelessly, do not use the range test functionality.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
\fB\fCubertooth\-util\fR exits with 0 when every requested operation succeeds, including
queries that report a nonzero value such as \fB\fC\-b\fR (board ID), \fB\fC\-c\fR (channel), or
\fB\fC\-a\fR (power amplifier level). \fB\fC\-m\fR with no range test result yet also exits 0
(see RANGE TEST). It exits with a nonzero status if any requested
operation fails, even when a later operation in the same command succeeds.
It exits with 1, without opening a device, when two or more mode options are
given (see DESCRIPTION), when \fB\fC\-z\fR is combined with a mode option, or when an
\fB\fC\-a\fR level is not a single digit 0\-7
(\fB\fCerror: PA level must be a single digit 0\-7 (got <value>)\fR)\.
The same holds
for any other option value that is not a whole decimal number in its range
(\fB\fC\-c\fR, \fB\fC\-C\fR, \fB\fC\-d\fR, \fB\fC\-l\fR, \fB\fC\-q\fR, \fB\fC\-z\fR), for example
\fB\fCerror: channel must be 0\-78 (got abc)\fR\.
It also exits with 1, without opening a device, when the command line contains
a word that is not an option, and names it: \fB\fCerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR\&.
\fB\fCubertooth\-util\fR takes no other arguments.
.PP
When a \fB\fC\-b\fR, \fB\fC\-c\fR, \fB\fC\-a\fR, \fB\fC\-z\fR, \fB\fC\-l\fR, \fB\fC\-d\fR, \fB\fC\-p\fR, \fB\fC\-s\fR, \fB\fC\-m\fR or \fB\fC\-9\fR read fails,
no value line is printed on standard output, so an error code is never shown as
a value; the other requested queries are still reported. A failed \fB\fC\-b\fR, \fB\fC\-c\fR, \fB\fC\-a\fR, \fB\fC\-z\fR, \fB\fC\-l\fR, \fB\fC\-d\fR, \fB\fC\-p\fR, \fB\fC\-s\fR, \fB\fC\-m\fR or \fB\fC\-9\fR read also prints
\fB\fCerror: <query> read failed\fR (for example \fB\fCerror: channel read failed\fR or
\fB\fCerror: board ID read failed\fR) on standard error, after any message from
libubertooth. When the board returns fewer bytes than the read needs, that is
the only line, with the counts added, for example
\fB\fCerror: channel read failed: board returned 0 of 2 bytes\fR\.
